actors: Agent runner | Lab IdP | This app

step: The agent asks for a token as itself
msg: Agent runner -> Lab IdP: POST /token, agent id + secret
text: The agent has its own client id and secret, registered at the IdP like
  lab 06's digest job. Nobody logs in for it. The runner is the program that
  holds those credentials and carries out whatever the agent decides.

step: The IdP records what kind of caller this is
note: Lab IdP: adds type + sponsor
text: The token will say `client_type: agent` and name a human `sponsor`, taken
  from the registration. Those claims are how everything downstream can tell an
  agent from a job, and find the person who answers for it.

step: The token comes back short-lived
msg: Lab IdP -> Agent runner: token, 2 min, 2 scopes
text: Agent tokens live two minutes, against five for an ordinary service.
  What an agent does next isn't fixed in advance, so a stolen token should
  be worth less.

step: The runner decides which tools the agent may even see
note: Agent runner: offers matching tools
text: The runner compares the token's scopes with its list of tools and offers
  only the ones that match. An agent can only ask for tools it knows about,
  so this is the first line of defence. The API is the second.

step: The agent picks an action and the runner calls the app
msg: Agent runner -> This app: GET /internal/stats + token
text: The agent chose `get_stats`. The runner makes the call, attaching the
  agent's bearer token. The agent itself never touches the credential.

step: The app asks the IdP about the token
msg: This app -> Lab IdP: POST /introspect
text: Same introspection as labs 05 and 06. The app trusts nothing in the
  request except what the IdP says about the token.

step: The IdP says what kind of caller it is
msg: Lab IdP -> This app: active + agent + sponsor
text: Along with `active` and the scopes, the answer now carries `client_type`,
  `client_name` and `sponsor`.

step: The app files the call under the right identity
note: This app: audit: agent for alice
msg: This app -> Agent runner: 200 + the numbers
text: The audit row says an agent did this, which agent, and who sponsors it.
  Misfile it as a plain service and the dashboard has nothing to show.

step: Minutes later, the runner refreshes before the token dies
note: Agent runner: token near expiry
text: The agent runs unattended, so no one is around to log in again. The
  runner checks the clock before each call and fetches a new token while the
  old one still has a little life left, instead of finding out from a 401.
